nTherefore, the best move is to discard B3. That way, I remove a tile that's not part of any potential chow or pung, and it's a middle tile with multiple copies left, reducing the chance of others needing it.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding B3 is the optimal choice as it is a middle tile with multiple copies remaining and not part of any potential chow or pung in my hand. Keeping W2, W3, W4 preserves the W2-3-4 chow possibility. Avoiding discarding honors (F2, F3) preserves potential Seat/Prevailing Wind points.]\nReason: [B3 is not part of any viable chow or pung in my hand and has 4 remaining copies, minimizing risk of aiding opponents. Discarding it maintains flexibility for future draws while preserving critical chow components (W2-3-4) and honors (F2/F3) for potential scoring.]\nAnswer: Play B3"}
