 (terminal)\n- T9 (terminal)\n- W9 (terminal)\n- F1, F2, J3 (honors)\n- T2, T6 (non-terminal)\n- W2, W6 (non-terminal)\n\nSo if I can discard T2, T6, W2, W6, then the remaining tiles would be T1, T9, W9, F1, F2, J3, plus the meld [W2, W3, W4]. Wait, but the meld includes W2, which is a non-terminal. So that would not qualify for All Terminals and Honors. Therefore, this pattern is not possible.\n\nAnother possibility: Mixed Straight. Needs three chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. I have one Chow [W2, W3, W4], which is not part of the required sequences. Not likely.\n\nPerhaps the best bet is to discard an honor tile (F1, F2, J3) to reduce the number of non-suited tiles and increase the chance of forming melds with suited tiles. Among these, J3 has only one left in the unshown tiles (J3:1), so if I discard J3, there's a chance someone else might take it, but since it's a single, they can't form a Pung. Similarly for F1 and F2.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a suited tile that's not part of any meld, but that might not be as good. For example, W6. But I have W6 in hand, and if I discard it, maybe someone can take it for a Chi. But I don't have the other tiles to form a Chow with W6.\n\nConsidering the valid actions, the best options are to discard F2, J3, or F1. Among these, J3 has only one left in the unshown tiles, so discarding it might be less safe, but since it's an honor tile, it's not part of any meld. F2 has three left, so safer to discard. Similarly for F1.\n\nBut the user's valid actions include Play F2. Let me check the unshown tiles for F2: F2 has 3 remaining. So there are three F2s left. Discarding F2 would be safe, as there are still three in the wall. Similarly, F1 has 2 left.\n\nBut what about the possibility of forming a Fully Concealed Hand? No, because I already have an exposed Chi meld.\n\nAnother consideration: if I discard a tile that could be useful for others, like T1 or T2, but I don't have the other tiles to form a Chow. For example, T1 is part of T1-T2-T3, but I don't have T3. So discarding T1 might not be helpful.\n\nGiven all this, the best move is to discard an honor tile that's not part of any potential meld. Among the options, F2 is a good candidate because there are three left, so it's less likely to be needed by others. Also, keeping F1 and J3 might be more valuable if they can form a pair later, but since I have only one of each, it's not possible.\n\nSo the answer would be to Play F2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a mix of suited and honor tiles. The meld [W2, W3, W4] is a Chow, and the remaining tiles include W2, W6, W9, T1, T2, T6, T9, F1, F2, J3. The best move is to discard an honor tile that is not part of any potential meld, as it does not hinder the formation of other patterns.  \nReason: Discarding F2 (East Wind) is optimal because it is an honor tile with three remaining in the unshown tiles, making it less critical to retain. This action reduces non-suited tiles and maintains flexibility for forming melds with suited tiles like W6, W9, T1, T2, T6, T9. Other honor tiles (F1, J3) are also viable but F2 has the highest availability in the wall.  \nAnswer: Play F2"}
